window.GKA.contentParts.push({ subject: "reading", grade: "g6", levels: [
  {"n":1,"name":"Academic vocabulary 4","skill":"Words for reading across subjects","speed":0.8,"words":[["analyze","Analyze the pattern."],["synthesize","Synthesize ideas from two sources."],["evaluate","Evaluate the argument."],["criteria","Set clear criteria."],["convey","Convey the meaning."],["coherent","A coherent paragraph."],["precise","Precise language."],["ambiguous","An ambiguous answer."],["implicit","An implicit message."],["explicit","An explicit rule."],["assess","Assess the damage."],["cite","Cite your sources."],["concise","A concise summary is short and clear."],["derive","Derive the meaning from context."],["infer","Infer the author's purpose."],["integrate","Integrate the quote into your sentence."],["interpret","Interpret the data."],["relevant","Only relevant facts belong here."],["sufficient","Sufficient evidence supports the claim."],["valid","A valid conclusion follows from the facts."]],"story":{"title":"The Science Fair Judge","text":"When Ms. Nakamura asked me to help judge the fourth-grade science fair, she gave me three criteria: a clear question, a fair test, and a coherent explanation. I had to evaluate each poster against them, not against how colorful it was. Some posters were explicit about every step. Others were ambiguous, leaving me to guess what the student had actually measured. The hardest part was the implicit message in my scores: a low number could crush a nine-year-old. So I wrote precise comments to convey exactly what to fix. By the end, I had to synthesize twenty posters into one ranking, and I understood why teachers take so long to grade.","questions":[{"q":"What were the three criteria?","o":["Clear question, fair test, coherent explanation","Color, size, neatness","Speed, cost, fun"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'ambiguous' mean?","o":["Unclear, open to more than one meaning","Very detailed","Colorful"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did the narrator write precise comments?","o":["To show exactly what to fix without crushing the student","To fill the page","Because it was required"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'synthesize' mean in the last sentence?","o":["Combine into one whole","Throw away","Copy"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Ambiguous Rubric","text":"The essay rubric said be precise, but the criteria were ambiguous: what counted as a coherent paragraph? Jamal decided to evaluate his own draft the way a judge would. He found that his thesis was implicit, hidden in the third sentence, so he made it explicit in the first. He synthesized two sources instead of quoting one. He cut every sentence that did not convey something new. When he analyzed the final draft against the rubric, it finally fit. The rubric had not changed. His reading of it had.","questions":[{"q":"What was wrong with the first draft's thesis?","o":["It was implicit","It was too long","It was missing"],"a":0},{"q":"What does synthesize mean?","o":["Combine sources into a new whole","Copy","Summarize one source"],"a":0},{"q":"What does ambiguous mean?","o":["Unclear, open to more than one meaning","Strict","Short"],"a":0},{"q":"What changed by the end?","o":["Jamal's reading of the rubric","The rubric","The teacher"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":3,"name":"Greek and Latin roots 2","skill":"Roots like bene, mal, cred, vid","speed":0.8,"words":[["benefit","A clear benefit."],["benevolent","A benevolent king."],["malfunction","A machine malfunction."],["malicious","A malicious rumor."],["credible","A credible witness."],["incredible","An incredible story."],["evident","The truth is evident."],["video","A short video."],["manual","Read the manual."],["manufacture","Factories manufacture cars."],["beneficial","Exercise is beneficial."],["malnourished","A malnourished plant wilts."],["malady","A malady is an illness."],["credit","Give credit to the author."],["discredit","Evidence can discredit a claim."],["evidence","Evidence is what you can see."],["visual","A visual aid."],["manuscript","A handwritten manuscript."],["manipulate","Manipulate the clay by hand."],["benefactor","A benefactor gives money."]],"story":{"title":"The Robot That Would Not Stop","text":"Our robotics team's robot had a malfunction the night before the competition: it would not stop spinning. The manual was no help. A benevolent engineer from a rival team walked over and asked to see the code. Some of my teammates worried she had malicious intent, but her reputation was credible, and she spotted the error in a minute: a loop with no exit. The benefit of her help was evident the next day, when our robot stopped exactly on the line. We filmed a thank-you video for her team. It felt incredible to lose first place to the people who helped us earn second.","questions":[{"q":"What does the root 'mal' mean in malfunction?","o":["Bad","Good","Hand"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did some teammates worry about the engineer?","o":["They thought she might have bad intentions","She was late","She was loud"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the bug?","o":["A loop with no exit","A dead battery","A loose wheel"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'evident' mean?","o":["Clear and obvious","Hidden","Expensive"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Benevolent Hacker","text":"The school network had a malfunction every Monday at eight. The tech teacher suspected something malicious. The evidence pointed to a student, which seemed incredible until the student came forward. It was not malicious at all. Priya had written a manual script to back up the library catalog, and it was accidentally overloading the server. Her intention was benevolent, and her fix, done by hand that afternoon, was credible proof of it. The video of her explanation became a lesson on roots: bene, mal, cred, vid, man.","questions":[{"q":"What does mal mean?","o":["Bad","Good","Hand"],"a":0},{"q":"What was Priya's intention?","o":["Benevolent","Malicious","Careless"],"a":0},{"q":"What does cred mean in credible?","o":["Believe","See","Make"],"a":0},{"q":"What root means hand?","o":["Man","Vid","Bene"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":6,"name":"Prefixes anti-, counter-, non-, semi-","skill":"Against, opposite, not, half","speed":0.7,"words":[["antibiotic","An antibiotic fights bacteria."],["antisocial","Antisocial behavior."],["counterargument","A counterargument."],["counterclockwise","Turn counterclockwise."],["nonfiction","A nonfiction book."],["nonsense","Pure nonsense."],["semicircle","Sit in a semicircle."],["semifinal","The semifinal match."],["antidote","An antidote to boredom."],["nonstop","A nonstop flight."],["antifreeze","Antifreeze stops ice."],["antiseptic","An antiseptic kills germs."],["counterpart","A counterpart is a matching person."],["counterattack","A counterattack answers an attack."],["nonprofit","A nonprofit does not earn profit."],["nonverbal","A nod is nonverbal."],["semiannual","A semiannual sale happens twice a year."],["semisweet","Semisweet chocolate."],["antonym","An antonym is an opposite."],["nonstick","A nonstick pan."]],"story":{"title":"The Semifinal","text":"Before the chess semifinal, I read a nonfiction book about famous games as an antidote to nerves. My coach warned me that my opponent liked to turn the board, so to speak, by playing counterclockwise openings that mirrored mine. Sitting in a semicircle, my teammates whispered counterarguments to every move I considered. I told them it was nonsense to plan twenty moves ahead; three was enough. The game ran nonstop for two hours. I lost, but not badly, and I did not become antisocial about it. My coach said losing a semifinal is the best antibiotic against overconfidence.","questions":[{"q":"What does the prefix 'semi' mean?","o":["Half","Against","Not"],"a":0},{"q":"What did the narrator read before the match?","o":["A nonfiction book about chess games","A novel","A comic"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'counterargument' mean?","o":["An argument against","A loud argument","A chess move"],"a":0},{"q":"What did the coach call losing a semifinal?","o":["The best medicine against overconfidence","A disaster","Nonsense"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Semicircle Strategy","text":"In the semifinal, Coach Ward drew a semicircle on the whiteboard. The other team, she said, plays nonstop. Our counterargument is patience. She predicted their counterclockwise rotation and planned an antidote: a slow, nonsense-free passing game. One player, usually antisocial on the bench, became the loudest voice. The game, like a nonfiction book, had no surprise ending, only a well-earned one. They won by two.","questions":[{"q":"What does semi mean in semifinal?","o":["Half","Against","Not"],"a":0},{"q":"What does counter mean in counterargument?","o":["Against or opposite","Twice","Half"],"a":0},{"q":"What does non mean?","o":["Not","Against","Half"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the antidote to the nonstop team?","o":["A slow passing game","Running faster","A timeout"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":8,"name":"Words with -ize, -ify, -ate","skill":"Verb-making suffixes","speed":0.8,"words":[["summarize","Summarize the chapter."],["organize","Organize your notes."],["prioritize","Prioritize your tasks."],["clarify","Clarify the rule."],["simplify","Simplify the fraction."],["justify","Justify the answer."],["demonstrate","Demonstrate the skill."],["illustrate","Illustrate the point."],["evaluate","Evaluate the plan."],["collaborate","Collaborate with peers."],["categorize","Categorize the samples."],["memorize","Memorize the poem."],["visualize","Visualize the ending."],["identify","Identify the main idea."],["classify","Classify the rocks."],["notify","Notify the office."],["communicate","Communicate clearly."],["participate","Participate in class."],["investigate","Investigate the cause."],["motivate","Motivate the team."]],"story":{"title":"The Group Project","text":"Our group project started as chaos. Four people, four ideas, zero plan. Priya suggested we prioritize: list every task and rank it. Jamal offered to organize the shared folder. I tried to clarify what the teacher actually wanted, which turned out to be simpler than we feared, so we simplified the plan to three slides. Each of us had to demonstrate one experiment and illustrate it with a diagram. When we had to justify our choices in the final presentation, we could, because we had evaluated every option. The teacher's comment was one word: collaborate. She underlined it twice, and it was the highest grade we had ever gotten together.","questions":[{"q":"What did Priya suggest?","o":["Prioritize the tasks","Quit the project","Work alone"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'clarify' mean?","o":["Make clear","Make longer","Decorate"],"a":0},{"q":"Why could they justify their choices?","o":["They had evaluated every option","The teacher told them to","They guessed"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the teacher's one-word comment?","o":["Collaborate","Excellent","Simplify"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"Organize, Then Collaborate","text":"The group project had collapsed into chaos until Rosa stepped in. First, summarize what we know, she said. Then prioritize: what must be done today? To clarify, she drew the plan on the board, and to simplify, she cut two steps. Each member had to justify a choice and demonstrate progress by Friday. The final poster illustrated everything with one diagram. The teacher's evaluation praised one thing above all: a group that had learned to collaborate instead of compete.","questions":[{"q":"What was Rosa's first step?","o":["Summarize what they knew","Draw the poster","Assign blame"],"a":0},{"q":"What does prioritize mean?","o":["Decide what matters most first","Delete","Copy"],"a":0},{"q":"What suffix is shared by clarify, simplify, and justify?","o":["-ify","-ize","-ate"],"a":0},{"q":"What did the teacher praise?","o":["Collaboration","Neat handwriting","The deadline"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":10,"name":"Connotation and tone","skill":"How word choice shapes feeling","speed":0.8,"words":[["assertive","An assertive leader."],["bossy","A bossy sibling."],["frugal","A frugal shopper."],["cheap","A cheap gift."],["inquisitive","An inquisitive child."],["prying","Prying questions."],["persistent","A persistent effort."],["stubborn","A stubborn refusal."],["relaxed","A relaxed attitude."],["lazy","A lazy afternoon."],["confident","A confident speaker."],["cocky","A cocky player taunts."],["thrifty","A thrifty shopper saves."],["miserly","A miserly man hoards."],["curious","A curious student asks."],["nosy","A nosy neighbor spies."],["determined","A determined runner finishes."],["obstinate","An obstinate mule will not move."],["easygoing","An easygoing friend."],["sluggish","A sluggish morning."]],"story":{"title":"Two Reviews of One Coach","text":"The school paper printed two reviews of the same soccer coach. The first called her assertive, persistent, and frugal with the team budget. The second called her bossy, stubborn, and cheap. Both reviewers watched the same practices; they simply chose words with different connotations. I interviewed the coach. She was inquisitive about my questions, not prying, and relaxed rather than lazy about the criticism. Tone, she said, is the reviewer's choice, not mine. Then she pointed at the trophy case. Persistent or stubborn, she said, the trophies look the same.","questions":[{"q":"Which pair shows the same trait with opposite connotations?","o":["Assertive and bossy","Relaxed and persistent","Frugal and inquisitive"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the story say tone is?","o":["The reviewer's choice","The coach's fault","A kind of trophy"],"a":0},{"q":"Which word has the negative connotation: frugal or cheap?","o":["Cheap","Frugal","Both are positive"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the coach's point about the trophies?","o":["Results are the same no matter the label","She wanted more trophies","Trophies are cheap"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"One Dog, Two Reviews","text":"The shelter posted two descriptions of the same dog. Volunteer A wrote: assertive, inquisitive, persistent, with a relaxed evening manner. Volunteer B wrote: bossy, prying, stubborn, and lazy after six. The facts matched perfectly. The tone did not. Within a day, families lined up for Volunteer A's dog and nobody asked about Volunteer B's. The shelter kept both posts up as a lesson on connotation, and the dog, named Biscuit, went home on Saturday.","questions":[{"q":"Which description had a positive tone?","o":["Volunteer A's","Volunteer B's","Both"],"a":0},{"q":"Which pair of words describe the same trait?","o":["Persistent and stubborn","Relaxed and prying","Assertive and lazy"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the lesson?","o":["Connotation shapes how readers feel","Dogs are lazy","Volunteers disagree"],"a":0},{"q":"What does prying mean?","o":["Nosy in a rude way","Curious in a good way","Sleepy"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":12,"name":"Shades of meaning 2","skill":"Precise verbs and adjectives","speed":0.7,"words":[["saunter","Saunter down the street."],["trudge","Trudge through snow."],["dash","Dash to the bus."],["murmur","Murmur a reply."],["bellow","Bellow across the field."],["glimpse","A quick glimpse."],["scrutinize","Scrutinize the contract."],["minuscule","A minuscule error."],["colossal","A colossal wave."],["adequate","An adequate meal."],["amble","Amble along the beach."],["stagger","Stagger under the weight."],["bolt","The horse will bolt."],["mutter","Mutter under your breath."],["shriek","A shriek of surprise."],["peer","Peer through the fog."],["gape","Gape at the fireworks."],["gigantic","A gigantic wave."],["meager","A meager meal."],["ample","Ample room."]],"story":{"title":"Three Walks to School","text":"On Monday I trudged to school through slush, late and miserable. On Wednesday I dashed, because the bus had left without me. On Friday I sauntered, since it was a half day and the sun was out. The same route, three different verbs. My English teacher asked us to scrutinize our word choices in a journal. A glimpse of a bird is not the same as a stare, and a minuscule mistake is not a colossal one. She murmured her praise rather than bellowing it, which made it feel bigger. Adequate, she wrote on my first draft. Precise, she wrote on my third.","questions":[{"q":"Which verb shows a slow, heavy walk?","o":["Trudge","Dash","Saunter"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'scrutinize' mean?","o":["Examine very closely","Skim quickly","Ignore"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did the murmured praise feel bigger?","o":["It was quiet and personal","It was loud","It was written"],"a":0},{"q":"What did the teacher's two comments show?","o":["The writing improved from adequate to precise","The teacher was unfair","The writing got worse"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"A Glimpse of the Comet","text":"On the first night, Kai sauntered to the hill and glimpsed only a minuscule smudge. On the second, he trudged up in the rain and saw nothing. On the third, he dashed up at dusk, and there it was: a colossal streak across half the sky. He did not bellow; he murmured, as if it might hear. His notes were adequate at first, then detailed as he scrutinized it through binoculars. Three walks, three words for walking, one comet.","questions":[{"q":"What does minuscule mean?","o":["Tiny","Bright","Far"],"a":0},{"q":"Which word means walked slowly with effort?","o":["Trudged","Dashed","Sauntered"],"a":0},{"q":"What does scrutinize mean?","o":["Examine closely","Ignore","Draw"],"a":0},{"q":"Which word is the opposite of colossal?","o":["Minuscule","Adequate","Dash"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":13,"name":"Figurative language 2","skill":"Allusion, oxymoron, understatement","speed":0.8,"words":[["allusion","An allusion to a famous story."],["oxymoron","Jumbo shrimp is an oxymoron."],["understatement","Saying 'a bit wet' in a flood is understatement."],["paradox","A puzzling paradox."],["pun","A clever pun."],["irony","Dramatic irony."],["satire","Satire mocks gently."],["euphemism","A gentle euphemism."],["cliché","Avoid a cliché."],["analogy","A helpful analogy."],["hyperbole","Hyperbole exaggerates."],["personification","Personification gives human traits."],["symbolism","A dove is symbolism for peace."],["juxtaposition","Juxtaposition places opposites side by side."],["foreshadowing","Foreshadowing hints at what comes."],["idiom","Break a leg is an idiom."],["metaphor","Time is a thief is a metaphor."],["simile","As quiet as snow is a simile."],["imagery","Imagery appeals to the senses."],["motif","A recurring motif of keys."]],"story":{"title":"The Gentle Giant of Room 12","text":"Our substitute teacher called himself a gentle giant, which my friend pointed out was nearly an oxymoron. He was six foot five and spoke in a whisper. When the fire alarm blared, he said, a bit loud, perhaps, which was an understatement. He loved puns; when we studied the Greek gods, he said Zeus was a real shock. He made an allusion to Icarus whenever someone bragged, and he described homework as an opportunity for growth, a euphemism nobody believed. His satire of our principal's announcements was so gentle the principal laughed too. By Friday we understood the paradox of the man: the quietest voice in the school was the one we listened to most.","questions":[{"q":"Why is 'gentle giant' close to an oxymoron?","o":["It pairs two ideas that seem opposite","It is a lie","It is a pun"],"a":0},{"q":"What is the understatement in the story?","o":["Calling a blaring alarm 'a bit loud'","Calling Zeus a shock","Mentioning Icarus"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did he mention Icarus when someone bragged?","o":["As an allusion to a story about flying too high","Because Icarus was a student","To change the subject"],"a":0},{"q":"What is the paradox at the end?","o":["The quietest voice was listened to the most","The teacher was short","Homework was fun"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"Jumbo Shrimp and Other Oxymorons","text":"Ms. Oduya served a plate of jumbo shrimp and asked what was wrong with the name. An oxymoron, said Lee: two opposite words joined. The class hunted for more: deafening silence, original copy, act naturally. Then she switched to puns and made an allusion to Shakespeare that only Ana caught. The cliché of the day was time flies, which she declared a tired metaphor. By the end, Marcus offered a paradox: the more they learned, the less they knew. She wrote it on the board and called it the understatement of the year.","questions":[{"q":"What is an oxymoron?","o":["Two opposite words joined","An exaggeration","A hidden reference"],"a":0},{"q":"Which is an oxymoron?","o":["Deafening silence","Time flies","Break a leg"],"a":0},{"q":"What is an allusion?","o":["A reference to another work","A joke","A lie"],"a":0},{"q":"What is a cliché?","o":["An overused expression","A new idea","A contradiction"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":16,"name":"Literary analysis words","skill":"Talk about stories like a critic","speed":0.7,"words":[["protagonist","The protagonist is the main character."],["antagonist","The antagonist opposes the hero."],["conflict","Internal conflict."],["foreshadowing","Clever foreshadowing."],["motif","A recurring motif."],["symbolism","Rich symbolism."],["characterization","Strong characterization."],["setting","A vivid setting."],["resolution","A satisfying resolution."],["genre","The mystery genre."],["theme","The theme is courage."],["tone","The tone is playful."],["mood","The mood is tense."],["climax","The climax is the turning point."],["exposition","The exposition introduces the setting."],["point of view","Point of view shapes the story."],["irony","Irony means the opposite of what is expected."],["flashback","A flashback shows the past."],["suspense","Suspense keeps readers guessing."],["narrator","The narrator tells the tale."]],"story":{"title":"Reading Like a Detective","text":"In the mystery genre, the protagonist is usually a detective, and the antagonist is whoever hides the truth. But our teacher showed us that the real conflict is often inside the detective: should I trust my gut or the evidence? She pointed to foreshadowing in chapter one, where a clock stops at midnight, and the motif of broken clocks that follows. The symbolism, she argued, is that time is running out for the truth. Characterization comes through small choices: the detective pays for a stranger's coffee. The setting, a foggy port city, hides as much as the villain does. By the resolution, we understood that a good mystery solves the crime and reveals the detective at the same time.","questions":[{"q":"In a mystery, who is usually the protagonist?","o":["The detective","The villain","The victim"],"a":0},{"q":"What is the motif in the example?","o":["Broken clocks","Coffee","Fog"],"a":0},{"q":"How is the detective characterized?","o":["Through small choices, like paying for a stranger's coffee","Through a long speech","Through a photo"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the resolution of a good mystery do?","o":["Solves the crime and reveals the detective","Only names the villain","Starts a new crime"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Antagonist Was the Weather","text":"In the novel the class read, the protagonist was a girl named Wren and the antagonist was not a person but a drought. The conflict began in chapter one with foreshadowing: cracked soil, a dry well. The motif of birds returned each time hope did. Symbolism was everywhere; the final rain was more than water. In the resolution, Wren's characterization shifted from stubborn to steady. The genre, the class decided, was realistic fiction with the heart of a fable, and the setting was a character all by itself.","questions":[{"q":"Who or what was the antagonist?","o":["A drought","Wren","A neighbor"],"a":0},{"q":"What foreshadowed the conflict?","o":["Cracked soil and a dry well","A party","A letter"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the recurring motif?","o":["Birds","Keys","Boats"],"a":0},{"q":"How did Wren change?","o":["From stubborn to steady","From brave to afraid","She did not change"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":19,"name":"Words from literature","skill":"Vocabulary from classic stories","speed":0.7,"words":[["melancholy","A melancholy tune."],["serene","A serene lake."],["ominous","An ominous sky."],["jubilant","A jubilant crowd."],["wistful","A wistful smile."],["treacherous","A treacherous path."],["benevolent","A benevolent stranger."],["desolate","A desolate field."],["radiant","A radiant morning."],["solemn","A solemn promise."],["forlorn","A forlorn puppy in the rain."],["luminous","A luminous moon."],["tranquil","A tranquil lake."],["foreboding","A foreboding sky."],["exuberant","An exuberant crowd."],["bleak","A bleak winter field."],["ethereal","Ethereal music."],["somber","A somber ceremony."],["resplendent","A resplendent gown."],["poignant","A poignant goodbye."]],"story":{"title":"The Lighthouse Keeper's Journal","text":"March 3. The sea is serene this morning, the sky radiant. I made a solemn promise to keep the light burning, and I keep it.\n\nMarch 9. An ominous bank of cloud sits on the horizon. The path to the village is treacherous with ice. I feel a little melancholy, alone with the gulls.\n\nMarch 15. The storm came and went. The beach is desolate, littered with weed and wood, but every ship made it home. A benevolent fisherman left bread at my door. I am wistful for company, yet jubilant too. The light held. That is the job.","questions":[{"q":"What does 'ominous' suggest about the clouds?","o":["Something bad is coming","They are pretty","They are small"],"a":0},{"q":"How does the keeper feel on March 9?","o":["A little sad and lonely","Jubilant","Angry"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'desolate' describe?","o":["The empty, littered beach","The fisherman","The light"],"a":0},{"q":"Why is the keeper jubilant at the end?","o":["Every ship made it home","The storm is coming","The bread was good"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"Entries From a Winter Journal","text":"December 3. The harbor is serene, the sky radiant, and I am wistful for summer already. December 9. An ominous wind. The gulls have gone. The water looks treacherous. December 15. A desolate week; the ferry did not come, and the house felt melancholy. December 20. The lighthouse keeper from the far point rowed over with bread. A benevolent man. We sat in solemn silence, then laughed. December 25. Jubilant. The ferry returned, and so did the gulls.","questions":[{"q":"What does ominous mean?","o":["Threatening","Calm","Bright"],"a":0},{"q":"Which entry has the saddest mood?","o":["December 15","December 3","December 25"],"a":0},{"q":"What does benevolent mean?","o":["Kind and generous","Loud","Lonely"],"a":0},{"q":"What does jubilant mean?","o":["Joyful","Tired","Worried"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":21,"name":"Argument vocabulary","skill":"Reading and writing arguments","speed":0.8,"words":[["thesis","A clear thesis."],["premise","A false premise."],["rebuttal","A strong rebuttal."],["concede","Concede a point."],["fallacy","A logical fallacy."],["credibility","Build credibility."],["objective","An objective report."],["subjective","A subjective opinion."],["warrant","The warrant links evidence to claim."],["nuance","Add nuance."],["claim","State your claim."],["counterclaim","Address the counterclaim."],["evidence","Evidence supports claims."],["reasoning","Explain your reasoning."],["bias","Bias slants a view."],["assumption","Check your assumption."],["logical","A logical argument."],["persuade","Persuade the council."],["refute","Refute the opposing view."],["valid","A valid point."]],"story":{"title":"The Phone Policy Debate","text":"Our thesis was that students should keep phones in lockers during class. The other side's premise was that phones are learning tools. We conceded that point; a phone can be a calculator or a dictionary. Our rebuttal was that the same device is also a distraction, and we cited an objective study, not a subjective complaint from a teacher. The other team used a fallacy: because one class used phones well, all classes would. We pointed out the warrant was missing: one example does not prove a rule. The judges praised our nuance. Neither side won outright, but our credibility grew because we admitted what was true on the other side.","questions":[{"q":"What does it mean to 'concede' a point?","o":["Admit the other side is right about it","Ignore it","Repeat it"],"a":0},{"q":"Which is objective evidence?","o":["A study","A teacher's complaint","A feeling"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the other team's fallacy?","o":["Using one example to prove a rule","Lying","Shouting"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did the team's credibility grow?","o":["They admitted what was true on the other side","They spoke loudly","They had more members"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Thesis Under the Bleachers","text":"The debate topic was whether the school should sell snacks at games. Diego's thesis was yes, with the premise that profits fund the band. Ana's rebuttal was that sugar sales contradicted the wellness policy. Diego conceded that point, then exposed a fallacy in her next one: she had argued that because one game had litter, all would. The judges weighed credibility, noting which claims were objective and which were subjective. The nuance that decided it came from Diego: fruit and water, sold by the band. Both sides clapped.","questions":[{"q":"What was Diego's premise?","o":["Profits fund the band","Snacks are healthy","Games are long"],"a":0},{"q":"What did Diego concede?","o":["Sugar sales contradicted the wellness policy","The band was bad","Litter was fine"],"a":0},{"q":"What fallacy did Ana make?","o":["One game with litter means all would","Quoting a fake expert","Changing the subject"],"a":0},{"q":"What does nuance mean?","o":["A subtle distinction","A loud claim","A rule"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":22,"name":"Words about reasoning","skill":"Logic words","speed":0.8,"words":[["deduce","Deduce the answer."],["infer","Infer the meaning."],["assumption","Check your assumption."],["contradict","The facts contradict the claim."],["consistent","A consistent story."],["plausible","A plausible explanation."],["verify","Verify the source."],["speculate","Do not speculate."],["rational","A rational decision."],["skeptical","A skeptical reader."],["hypothesis","Form a hypothesis."],["evidence","Gather evidence."],["conclusion","Draw a conclusion."],["logic","Follow the logic."],["premise","A premise is a starting point."],["bias","Watch for bias."],["valid","A valid argument."],["correlation","Correlation is not causation."],["prove","Prove it with data."],["doubt","Reasonable doubt."]],"story":{"title":"The Missing Trophy","text":"When the debate trophy vanished from the display case, the school buzzed with speculation. A skeptical librarian asked us to be rational. First, we listed our assumptions: that it was stolen, that it happened overnight. Then we tried to verify them. The security log contradicted the overnight theory; the case had been opened at 2:15 p.m. From the janitor's consistent story, we could deduce that the case was unlocked for cleaning. We inferred that someone had moved the trophy, not stolen it. The plausible explanation turned out to be true: the principal had taken it to be engraved. The lesson was not about trophies. It was about checking assumptions before telling a story.","questions":[{"q":"What contradicted the overnight theory?","o":["The security log","The janitor","The principal"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'deduce' mean?","o":["Reach a conclusion from evidence","Guess wildly","Deny"],"a":0},{"q":"Where was the trophy?","o":["Being engraved","In a locker","Stolen"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the lesson?","o":["Check assumptions before telling a story","Lock the case","Trust nobody"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Case of the Wet Floor","text":"The gym floor was wet, and the coach assumed a leak. Nadia was skeptical. She deduced from the puddle's shape that it came from the door, not the ceiling. A plausible explanation was rain blown in, but the forecast had been dry, which contradicted it. She speculated about a water bottle, then verified by finding one tipped in the bleachers. Her assumption that someone was careless was consistent with the evidence, and her conclusion was rational. The coach, to his credit, apologized to the roof.","questions":[{"q":"What did the coach assume?","o":["A leak","A spill","Rain"],"a":0},{"q":"What does deduce mean?","o":["Reach a conclusion from evidence","Guess","Ignore"],"a":0},{"q":"What contradicted the rain explanation?","o":["The forecast was dry","The door was shut","The roof was new"],"a":0},{"q":"How did Nadia verify her idea?","o":["Found a tipped water bottle","Asked the coach","Checked the weather"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":26,"name":"Science vocabulary","skill":"Words from middle school science","speed":0.8,"words":[["molecule","A water molecule."],["photosynthesis","Plants use photosynthesis."],["velocity","The car's velocity."],["density","Oil has low density."],["catalyst","A catalyst speeds a reaction."],["equilibrium","Reach equilibrium."],["conductor","Copper is a conductor."],["insulator","Rubber is an insulator."],["hypothesis","A testable hypothesis."],["data","Collect the data."],["atom","An atom is the smallest unit of an element."],["compound","Water is a compound."],["reaction","A chemical reaction."],["friction","Friction slows motion."],["gravity","Gravity pulls objects down."],["mass","Mass measures matter."],["energy","Energy is the ability to do work."],["variable","Change one variable."],["solution","Salt water is a solution."],["evaporate","Puddles evaporate."]],"story":{"title":"The Density Tower","text":"For the science expo, we built a density tower: honey, dish soap, water, oil, and alcohol stacked in a glass without mixing. The hypothesis was simple, that liquids with higher density sink below liquids with lower density. The data proved it, but the surprise came when a visitor dropped a grape in. It stopped at the water layer, in a kind of equilibrium between sinking and floating. A younger kid asked if the grape was a conductor or an insulator. Not that kind of science, my partner said, but she liked the question. She explained molecules and even photosynthesis before the kid's mother steered him away. We measured the velocity of nothing that day, but we learned that curiosity is the best catalyst.","questions":[{"q":"Why did the liquids stack without mixing?","o":["They have different densities","They were cold","They were colored"],"a":0},{"q":"What happened to the grape?","o":["It stopped at the water layer","It sank to the bottom","It floated on top"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'equilibrium' mean here?","o":["A balance between sinking and floating","A kind of grape","A measurement"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the 'best catalyst'?","o":["Curiosity","Honey","The visitor"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Catalyst in Room 7","text":"The experiment measured how fast a sugar cube dissolved. The hypothesis: hot water wins. The data agreed, but a question arose about why. Lin explained that heat makes molecules move faster, so the water reaches equilibrium with the sugar sooner. Omar asked if stirring was a catalyst. Not quite, Lin said; a catalyst is a substance that speeds a reaction without being used up. The spoon is a conductor of motion, not a catalyst. Their report ended with a clear velocity graph and a cleaner definition than the textbook's.","questions":[{"q":"What was the hypothesis?","o":["Hot water dissolves sugar faster","Cold water is better","Stirring does nothing"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does heat speed dissolving?","o":["Molecules move faster","Sugar gets lighter","Water gets denser"],"a":0},{"q":"What is a catalyst?","o":["A substance that speeds a reaction without being used up","A spoon","A kind of sugar"],"a":0},{"q":"What does equilibrium mean?","o":["A balanced state","A fast change","A measurement tool"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":30,"name":"Social studies vocabulary","skill":"Words from history and geography","speed":0.8,"words":[["civilization","An ancient civilization."],["empire","A vast empire."],["monarchy","A monarchy has a king or queen."],["republic","A republic elects leaders."],["treaty","Sign a treaty."],["migration","A great migration."],["agriculture","Agriculture feeds nations."],["trade route","An old trade route."],["artifact","A clay artifact."],["archaeologist","An archaeologist digs."],["dynasty","A dynasty is a ruling family."],["pharaoh","A pharaoh ruled Egypt."],["democracy","Athens had a democracy."],["senate","The Roman senate made laws."],["irrigation","Irrigation waters crops."],["nomad","A nomad moves with the seasons."],["currency","Coins are a currency."],["monument","A monument honors the past."],["scribe","A scribe wrote records."],["conquest","A conquest takes land."]],"story":{"title":"The Shoebox Dig","text":"Our teacher buried artifacts in shoeboxes of sand and made us archaeologists for a week. My box held a clay bead, a bent coin, and a scrap of woven cloth. From those clues, our group had to infer the civilization that made them. The coin suggested trade routes; the cloth suggested agriculture, since someone had grown the fiber. A map in the box showed a river, and we argued about whether the people were a monarchy or a republic. We decided we could not know from three objects. On Friday, each group signed a treaty to share findings. The migration of ideas between boxes, our teacher said, was the real lesson.","questions":[{"q":"What did the coin suggest?","o":["Trade routes","A monarchy","Agriculture"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did the group decide they could not know the government type?","o":["Three objects were not enough evidence","The map was missing","They ran out of time"],"a":0},{"q":"What is an archaeologist?","o":["Someone who studies the past through objects","A map maker","A king"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the 'real lesson'?","o":["Sharing ideas between groups","Digging carefully","Signing treaties"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Trade Route in the Hallway","text":"To understand how an empire grew, the class built a trade route down the hallway. Each classroom was a civilization. The monarchy in Room 3 taxed every artifact that passed. The republic in Room 5 signed a treaty with Room 6 to share agriculture. Migration happened when Room 4 ran out of beads. The archaeologist of the day, Mrs. Han, dug through the lost-and-found box at the end and reconstructed the entire history from what people had dropped.","questions":[{"q":"What did the monarchy in Room 3 do?","o":["Taxed every artifact","Shared beads","Signed a treaty"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did migration happen?","o":["Room 4 ran out of beads","A flood","A war"],"a":0},{"q":"What does an archaeologist do?","o":["Reconstructs the past from objects","Signs treaties","Rules an empire"],"a":0},{"q":"What is a treaty?","o":["A formal agreement","A tax","A road"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":33,"name":"Words about government and law","skill":"Civics vocabulary","speed":0.7,"words":[["legislation","New legislation."],["amendment","A constitutional amendment."],["jurisdiction","Outside their jurisdiction."],["veto","The governor can veto."],["majority","A majority vote."],["candidate","A candidate for mayor."],["campaign","A long campaign."],["ballot","Mark the ballot."],["judicial","The judicial branch."],["civic","A civic duty."],["democracy","A democracy is rule by the people."],["constitution","The constitution is the highest law."],["citizenship","Citizenship brings rights and duties."],["election","An election chooses leaders."],["representative","A representative speaks for voters."],["bill","A bill becomes a law."],["court","The court decides cases."],["rights","Rights are protected freedoms."],["petition","Sign the petition."],["debate","Debate the issue."]],"story":{"title":"Student Council Week","text":"During student council week, three candidates ran a campaign for president. The ballot also had an amendment: should the council meet weekly instead of monthly? It passed by a clear majority. The new president tried to veto the lunch committee's plan for taco Tuesdays, but the council's rules did not give her that power; it was outside her jurisdiction. A teacher acting as the judicial branch settled the dispute by reading the rules aloud. Then the council passed legislation about recycling bins. My civic duty, as a sixth grader, was mostly to vote and to stop arguing about tacos.","questions":[{"q":"What was the amendment about?","o":["Meeting weekly instead of monthly","Tacos","Recycling"],"a":0},{"q":"Why could the president not veto the taco plan?","o":["It was outside her jurisdiction","She was absent","The majority agreed"],"a":0},{"q":"Who acted as the judicial branch?","o":["A teacher","The president","The candidates"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'legislation' mean?","o":["Laws or rules that are passed","A campaign","A vote"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Recess Amendment","text":"Student Council took up the recess question. Mia proposed legislation to add ten minutes. Jonah's amendment limited it to Fridays. The civic lesson came when the council president tried to veto the vote, and the advisor explained that jurisdiction over the schedule belonged to the principal, not the council. So the candidates for the next election ran a campaign on a single promise: a real ballot on recess. The majority of students voted. The judicial review, as the advisor called it, took place in the principal's office, and Fridays got longer.","questions":[{"q":"What did Jonah's amendment do?","o":["Limited the extra recess to Fridays","Cancelled recess","Added an hour"],"a":0},{"q":"Why couldn't the council president veto?","o":["The schedule was outside the council's jurisdiction","The vote was tied","The advisor said no"],"a":0},{"q":"What does majority mean?","o":["More than half","Everyone","A few"],"a":0},{"q":"What does a ballot do?","o":["Records a vote","Makes a law","Starts a campaign"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":35,"name":"Words about technology and media","skill":"Vocabulary for a digital world","speed":0.7,"words":[["algorithm","An algorithm sorts the feed."],["source","Check the source."],["credible","A credible site."],["misinformation","Spot misinformation."],["privacy","Protect your privacy."],["digital","A digital footprint."],["evaluate","Evaluate the claim."],["cite","Cite your sources."],["plagiarism","Plagiarism is copying."],["network","A social network."],["password","Keep your password secret."],["encrypt","Encrypt the message."],["browser","Open the browser."],["database","A database stores records."],["upload","Upload the file."],["bandwidth","Low bandwidth slows video."],["attribution","Give attribution to the creator."],["fact-check","Fact-check before sharing."],["clickbait","Clickbait headlines mislead."],["footprint","Your digital footprint lasts."]],"story":{"title":"The Viral Video","text":"A video claiming that a nearby lake had turned purple went viral in our town. My class decided to evaluate it. First, we traced the source: an account with no name and no other posts, which did not seem credible. Then we checked the lake ourselves. It was green, as always. The algorithm had spread the misinformation because people shared it, not because it was true. We wrote a short report, cited two local news sources, and posted it to the school network. Our teacher reminded us that a digital footprint lasts, so we kept it polite, and we respected the privacy of the account by not naming it. Avoiding plagiarism was easy: we had done the work ourselves.","questions":[{"q":"Why did the source seem not credible?","o":["It had no name and no other posts","It was too old","It was from a newspaper"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did the algorithm spread the video?","o":["People shared it","It was true","The lake was purple"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'cite' mean?","o":["Name the sources you used","Copy","Delete"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did the class not name the account?","o":["To respect its privacy","They forgot","It was secret"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Source Behind the Headline","text":"A headline claimed the school would close for a month. It spread through the network in an hour. Amir was asked to evaluate it for the student paper. The source was a site with no author and no date; its credibility was low. He traced the claim and found the original: a satire page. Misinformation, he wrote, is an algorithm's favorite food. He cited two real sources, protected the privacy of the student who had shared it first, and avoided plagiarism by quoting with attribution. The digital correction reached fewer people than the rumor. It usually does.","questions":[{"q":"Why was the source's credibility low?","o":["No author and no date","It was too long","It had pictures"],"a":0},{"q":"Where did the claim come from?","o":["A satire page","The principal","A news channel"],"a":0},{"q":"How did Amir avoid plagiarism?","o":["Quoted with attribution","Did not write anything","Changed one word"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the last line suggest?","o":["Corrections spread less than rumors","Everyone read the correction","Rumors are rare"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":37,"name":"Words about health and the body","skill":"Health vocabulary","speed":0.7,"words":[["nutrition","Good nutrition."],["metabolism","A fast metabolism."],["immune","The immune system."],["cardiovascular","Cardiovascular exercise."],["respiratory","The respiratory system."],["hydration","Stay hydrated."],["fatigue","Fight fatigue."],["stamina","Build stamina."],["posture","Good posture."],["wellness","A wellness plan."],["calorie","A calorie measures energy in food."],["protein","Protein builds muscle."],["vitamin","Vitamin C is in oranges."],["digestion","Digestion breaks down food."],["pulse","Check your pulse."],["oxygen","Lungs take in oxygen."],["flexibility","Stretching improves flexibility."],["endurance","Endurance means lasting long."],["recovery","Rest helps recovery."],["balance","A balanced diet."]],"story":{"title":"Training for the 5K","text":"When I signed up for the charity 5K, I thought running was just running. My aunt, a nurse, explained the science. Cardiovascular exercise strengthens the heart; the respiratory system learns to move more air; and nutrition, especially breakfast, feeds the metabolism. The first week, fatigue hit me at the one-mile mark. By week four, my stamina had doubled. She corrected my posture so my lungs could fill, and she nagged me about hydration until I carried a bottle everywhere. On race day I finished in thirty-one minutes, tired but not sick, because, she said, a healthy immune system is part of wellness too.","questions":[{"q":"What does cardiovascular exercise strengthen?","o":["The heart","The bones","The eyes"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'stamina' mean?","o":["The ability to keep going","Speed","Weight"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did the aunt correct the narrator's posture?","o":["So the lungs could fill","To look taller","For a photo"],"a":0},{"q":"What is 'hydration'?","o":["Drinking enough water","Sleeping","Stretching"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"Hydration Versus Hope","text":"Noor trained for the swim meet by hoping. Her coach replaced hope with a plan: nutrition before practice, hydration during, and sleep after, because the immune system and metabolism both depend on rest. Her stamina rose week by week. Fatigue still came, but later each time. She learned that posture in the water changed her respiratory rhythm and that her cardiovascular fitness showed up as a lower resting pulse. On meet day, wellness looked like a simple thing: a bottle of water and a full night of sleep.","questions":[{"q":"What replaced hope?","o":["A plan","A new coach","A bigger pool"],"a":0},{"q":"What does stamina mean?","o":["Ability to keep going","Speed","Strength"],"a":0},{"q":"What showed her improved cardiovascular fitness?","o":["A lower resting pulse","Longer arms","Faster eating"],"a":0},{"q":"What does hydration mean?","o":["Taking in enough water","Sleeping","Stretching"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":39,"name":"Words about art and music","skill":"Vocabulary of the arts","speed":0.7,"words":[["composition","A balanced composition."],["perspective","Perspective adds depth."],["texture","Rough texture."],["palette","A warm palette."],["tempo","A fast tempo."],["harmony","Sweet harmony."],["improvise","Improvise a melody."],["rehearse","Rehearse daily."],["critique","A fair critique."],["medium","Oil paint is a medium."],["sketch","A quick sketch."],["contrast","Contrast of light and dark."],["melody","A catchy melody."],["rhythm","Keep the rhythm."],["sculpture","A marble sculpture."],["gallery","An art gallery."],["chorus","Sing the chorus."],["solo","A violin solo."],["canvas","Paint on canvas."],["symmetry","The design has symmetry."]],"story":{"title":"The Jazz Band and the Mural","text":"Our school's jazz band rehearsed next to the art room, so the two classes decided to collaborate. The painters chose a warm palette and a composition with strong perspective: a street that seemed to run into the wall. The band chose a fast tempo for the opening and a slower section in harmony. On the day of the show, the band improvised while the painters added texture with sponges. A visiting artist gave a critique: the music and the painting told the same story in two mediums. Nobody had planned that. It happened because two rooms shared a wall and decided to listen through it.","questions":[{"q":"What does 'perspective' do in a painting?","o":["Adds depth","Adds color","Adds sound"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'improvise' mean?","o":["Make it up as you go","Follow the sheet music","Stop playing"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the artist's critique?","o":["The music and painting told the same story","The colors were wrong","The tempo was too fast"],"a":0},{"q":"What is a 'medium' in art?","o":["The material used to make art","A size","A price"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"Critique Night","text":"At critique night, the art club and the jazz band swapped vocabularies. The painters explained composition and perspective, how a palette of three colors could carry texture. The musicians answered with tempo and harmony, and demonstrated how to improvise over four chords. Each group had to rehearse the other's medium for one week. The painters' concert was messy and brave. The band's mural had a wobbly trumpet and perfect rhythm. The critique at the end was kind, specific, and honest, the way a good critique is.","questions":[{"q":"What did the painters explain?","o":["Composition and perspective","Tempo and harmony","Scales"],"a":0},{"q":"What does improvise mean?","o":["Create on the spot","Copy exactly","Rehearse"],"a":0},{"q":"What is a medium in art?","o":["The material or form used","A size","A price"],"a":0},{"q":"What makes a good critique?","o":["Kind, specific, and honest","Loud","Short"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":41,"name":"Words about environment and climate","skill":"Earth science vocabulary","speed":0.7,"words":[["sustainable","A sustainable farm."],["renewable","Renewable energy."],["conservation","Water conservation."],["biodiversity","Rich biodiversity."],["emission","Carbon emission."],["habitat","A protected habitat."],["pollution","Air pollution."],["drought","A long drought."],["erosion","Soil erosion."],["ecosystem","A fragile ecosystem."],["recycle","Recycle paper and cans."],["compost","Compost food scraps."],["carbon","Carbon dioxide traps heat."],["solar","Solar panels catch sunlight."],["wildlife","Protect wildlife."],["deforestation","Deforestation removes trees."],["glacier","A glacier melts slowly."],["footprint","Reduce your carbon footprint."],["wetland","A wetland filters water."],["species","An endangered species."]],"story":{"title":"The Creek Restoration","text":"Behind our school, a creek had turned brown from erosion and pollution. Our science class joined a conservation group to restore it. We planted native grasses on the banks to stop erosion, and the biodiversity returned within a season: dragonflies, frogs, a heron. During the summer drought, the deeper pools we dug kept the ecosystem alive. The group used a solar pump, a renewable source with no emissions, to move water. A sustainable project, our teacher said, is one you could keep doing forever. We agreed the creek was now a habitat worth the muddy boots.","questions":[{"q":"Why did they plant native grasses?","o":["To stop erosion","For decoration","To feed the heron"],"a":0},{"q":"What showed that biodiversity returned?","o":["Dragonflies, frogs, and a heron appeared","The water got warmer","The pump worked"],"a":0},{"q":"Why was the solar pump a good choice?","o":["It is renewable with no emissions","It was cheap","It was loud"],"a":0},{"q":"What does 'sustainable' mean according to the teacher?","o":["Something you could keep doing forever","Something expensive","Something fast"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Rain Garden","text":"The parking lot flooded every spring, carrying pollution into the creek. The science club proposed a sustainable fix: a rain garden to slow runoff and stop erosion. They chose native plants to support biodiversity and the local habitat. The emission of one truck, they calculated, was offset by the trees they planted along the edge. The conservation grant covered the soil. During the summer drought, the garden survived with renewable rainwater stored in barrels. By fall, frogs had moved in, which the club counted as the ecosystem's approval.","questions":[{"q":"What was the problem?","o":["Flooding carried pollution to the creek","No parking","Too many frogs"],"a":0},{"q":"Why choose native plants?","o":["To support biodiversity and habitat","They were cheap","They were tall"],"a":0},{"q":"What does sustainable mean?","o":["Able to continue without harming the future","Expensive","Temporary"],"a":0},{"q":"How did the garden survive the drought?","o":["Rainwater stored in barrels","A hose","It did not"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":43,"name":"Words about the mind","skill":"Thinking and feeling vocabulary","speed":0.7,"words":[["perceive","Perceive the change."],["conscious","A conscious choice."],["intuition","Trust your intuition."],["concentrate","Concentrate on the task."],["reflect","Reflect on the day."],["empathy","Show empathy."],["resilience","Resilience after failure."],["perspective","Keep perspective."],["mindset","A growth mindset."],["motivation","Find motivation."],["attention","Pay attention."],["memory","A good memory."],["curiosity","Curiosity drives learning."],["focus","Focus on one task."],["habit","A daily habit."],["instinct","An instinct is natural."],["logic","Use logic."],["imagination","A vivid imagination."],["patience","Patience pays off."],["confidence","Confidence grows with practice."]],"story":{"title":"The Growth Mindset Experiment","text":"Our teacher ran an experiment on us. For two weeks, we had to reflect each evening on one thing we failed at and one thing we learned from it. At first it felt silly. Then I began to perceive a change: a math test I would once have dreaded became a puzzle I could concentrate on. She called it a growth mindset, the conscious choice to see ability as something that grows. My intuition said it would not work for everyone, and it did not; my friend needed empathy more than advice. But resilience, I learned, is not pretending you did not fail. It is keeping perspective long enough to find the motivation to try again.","questions":[{"q":"What did students have to do each evening?","o":["Reflect on a failure and what they learned","Do extra homework","Write a poem"],"a":0},{"q":"What is a growth mindset?","o":["Seeing ability as something that grows","Being tall","Always winning"],"a":0},{"q":"What did the friend need?","o":["Empathy more than advice","A tutor","A new teacher"],"a":0},{"q":"How does the narrator define resilience?","o":["Keeping perspective to try again","Never failing","Ignoring failure"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Week Without Phones","text":"For one week the class agreed to concentrate without phones. At first, everyone was conscious of the empty pocket every few minutes. By Wednesday, students began to perceive small things: the hum of the lights, a classmate's mood. Intuition got sharper. When they reflected on Friday, the mindset had shifted. Empathy, one student wrote, is easier when you are looking at a face. Resilience, another wrote, is surviving boredom. The motivation to try a second week came from an unexpected perspective: the teacher, who admitted it had been hardest for her.","questions":[{"q":"What did students notice by Wednesday?","o":["Small things like sounds and moods","Nothing","Their phones"],"a":0},{"q":"What does perceive mean?","o":["Notice or become aware of","Ignore","Remember"],"a":0},{"q":"What did one student say about empathy?","o":["It is easier when looking at a face","It is impossible","It needs a phone"],"a":0},{"q":"Who found it hardest?","o":["The teacher","The students","The principal"],"a":0}]}},
  {"n":49,"name":"Reading a longer text","skill":"Theme, structure, and author's purpose","speed":0.6,"words":[["purpose","The author's purpose."],["structure","Text structure."],["theme","A universal theme."],["inference","A careful inference."],["juxtaposition","Juxtaposition of rich and poor."],["ambiguity","The ending's ambiguity."],["perspective","A shift in perspective."],["tension","Rising tension."],["epiphany","A sudden epiphany."],["legacy","A lasting legacy."],["foreshadowing","Foreshadowing hints at later events."],["irony","Irony twists expectation."],["symbolism","Symbolism gives objects meaning."],["motif","A motif repeats."],["climax","The climax is the peak."],["resolution","The resolution ties up the story."],["allegory","An allegory has a hidden meaning."],["paradox","A paradox seems to contradict itself."],["catharsis","Catharsis releases emotion."],["archetype","The hero is an archetype."]],"story":{"title":"The Bridge Builder of Lanos","text":"In the valley town of Lanos, the river split the community in two. The east bank had the school; the west bank had the mill where most parents worked. For decades, children crossed on a rope ferry that ran only when the water was low. The town argued about a bridge every spring and forgot about it every summer.\n\nElena Vargas was fourteen when her brother missed a month of school because of high water. She did not write a petition. She built a model. Using her father's woodworking tools and a library book on trusses, she constructed a bridge two meters long that held a bucket of rocks. She carried it to the town meeting and set it on the table without a speech.\n\nThe juxtaposition of a child's model next to the adults' stack of excuses created a tension nobody could ignore. The mayor had an epiphany, or at least he said so later: the town had been debating whether, when the only real question was how. The bridge was finished in two years. Elena became an engineer, but her legacy in Lanos is simpler. On the east end of the bridge, a small plaque reads: Built because a child stopped asking.","questions":[{"q":"What was the author's purpose?","o":["To show how one determined person can change a community","To explain how to build trusses","To describe a river"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the juxtaposition at the meeting?","o":["A child's working model beside adults' excuses","East and west banks","A bridge and a ferry"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the mayor's epiphany?","o":["The question was how, not whether","The town was broke","Elena was an engineer"],"a":0},{"q":"What can you infer from the plaque's words?","o":["Action mattered more than more debate","Children should not speak","The bridge was cheap"],"a":0},{"q":"How is the text structured?","o":["Problem, action, and result in chronological order","A list of facts","A poem"],"a":0}]},"story2":{"title":"The Map Seller of Quarry Street","text":"The map seller on Quarry Street sold maps of places that did not exist. Tourists bought them as jokes. Locals did not buy them at all. Idris, who delivered her bread each Tuesday, assumed she was a harmless eccentric until the morning he found the shop open, the seller gone, and a map on the counter with his own name written across the top. Its structure was ordinary: a key, a compass, a scale. Its content was not. It showed Quarry Street, but with the quarry itself drawn as a lake, the old mill as a library, and the empty lot where his grandfather's house had burned marked simply: garden. He carried it for a week before he understood. The map was not a place that did not exist. It was a place that did not exist yet. The juxtaposition of the street he knew and the street she had drawn became, for him, a kind of instruction. The ambiguity of the final mark, garden, never resolved; she never returned to explain. But the tension between the two maps, hers and the real one, became the purpose of his next ten years. The epiphany came late, when the lake was dug and the library opened: her legacy was not maps. It was the people who read them and started digging.","questions":[{"q":"What did the map seller sell?","o":["Maps of places that did not exist","Bread","Old photographs"],"a":0},{"q":"What was unusual about the map Idris found?","o":["It had his name and showed the street changed","It was torn","It was blank"],"a":0},{"q":"What is the story's theme?","o":["A vision of what could be can shape what is","Maps are useless","Streets never change"],"a":0},{"q":"What does juxtaposition refer to here?","o":["The real street placed beside the imagined one","A compass","A delivery"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the map seller's legacy?","o":["People who read her maps and started building","A famous shop","A lake"],"a":0}]}}
] });
